    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d159">

                <pb facs="0168" n="164" xml:id="pg164"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">651.50/164: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in France</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Bullitt</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">[Extract]<anchor n="38" xml:id="d159-f1"/></seg>
                </opener>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Paris</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1938-05-09T19:00:00+01:00">May 9, 1938—7 p.m.</date><lb/>[Received
                        May 10—12:40 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>739.…</p>
                <p rendition="#center">. . . . . . . . . . . . . .</p>
                <p>5. China.</p>
                <p>Daladier<anchor n="39" xml:id="d159-f2"/> said that he felt the resistance of the
                    Chinese to the Japanese was a magnificent and heartening example of courage and
                    patriotism. As soon as he had become Prime Minister he had given orders to the
                    French authorities in Indo-China to open the railroad completely to all
                    shipments of planes and munitions to China. The Japanese had protested
                    frequently and vigorously, and a Japanese Consul in Indo-China had gone so far
                    as to say to the French General there that if the shipment of munitions through
                    Indo-China should not be stopped Japan might have to make war. The French
                    General had replied: “Come on and see what happens to you!” This had produced a
                    very salutary effect. The Japanese Consul had left at once for Japan and had not
                    returned.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Bullitt</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d159-f1" n="38" xml:id="d159-f1-content">
                <p>For text of sections 2 and 3 of this telegram, see <ref
                        target="frus1938v01#pg_493">Vol. <hi rendition="#small-caps">i</hi>, pp.
                        493</ref> and <ref target="frus1938v01#pg_192">192</ref>.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d159-f2" n="39" xml:id="d159-f2-content">
                <p>Edouard Daladier, President of the French Council of Ministers.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
